    "result_text": "Terms can now be set per grade, and each claim's due-by is fixed when the claim is created. `bin/e2e` builds and passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**How it works now**\n- **Per-grade terms:** the settings form has a terms field for each of grades A, B and C (`setting-terms-<grade>`), and shows the value in force next to it (`setting-terms-<grade>-current`). Leaving a grade blank means it uses the sheet's terms, and blank is also how you clear it. Each save writes `SETTING_CHANGED \u2026 key=terms-<grade> value=<days>` to the audit file, right after the sheet's own `key=terms` line.\n- **Due-by fixed at creation:** when a claim is added (including through quick-add), its due-by is set to its date plus the terms for the grade its person has on that date. If that grade has no terms of its own, the sheet's terms are used. Changing the terms or the claim's date later doesn't move it. The server sends the stored due-by to the screen, and both the overdue checks and the late charge now use it instead of recalculating from the current terms.\n\n**Changes you might not expect**\n- **Changing the sheet's terms no longer adds ROLLUP/TOTALS audit lines.** It can't move any figure now, so it only writes the `SETTING_CHANGED` line.\n- **Existing claims got a one-off due-by.** A new database migration (`V30__grade_terms_and_due_by.sql`) gives each existing claim its date plus the sheet's terms at the moment the migration runs.\n- **\"Terms in force on the claim's date\" means the terms at creation.** Terms have no from-date, so the only date-dependent part is which grade the person has on the claim's date.\n- **I deleted `sheet-terms.ts`.** It held the sheet's terms for the screen to calculate due-bys, which it no longer does.\n\nThe main files changed are `Sheet.java`, `Claim.java`, `ClaimService.java`, `SettingService.java`, `SettingController.java` and `RollupService.java` on the server, and `settings.ts`, `claims-api.ts`, `claims.ts` and `people.ts` in the front end.",
